\details{
\code{log(RT - ndt)} follows a \strong{Student-t} distribution with location \code{mu},
scale \code{sigma} and \code{dof} degrees of freedom. As \code{dof} grows the Student-t
becomes the Normal, so \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}} is the \code{dof -> Inf} limit: this
family varies \strong{kurtosis} where \code{\link[=cogmod_loggamma]{cogmod_loggamma()}} varies skew.

\code{dof} is what \code{brms::student()} calls \code{nu}. It is renamed here because
\code{\link[=cogmod_lnr]{cogmod_lnr()}} already spends \code{nuzero} and \code{nuone} on drift rates, and
because \code{brms} recognises the name \code{nu} and supplies opinionated defaults for
it; \code{dof} arrives flat like every other parameter this package defines, so
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} simply fills it.

\code{ndt} and \code{poutlier} mean exactly what they do in \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}},
and \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} and
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} all work here too. See \code{?rcogmod_lognormal} for why \code{ndt} is
expressed directly in seconds rather than as a fraction of the fastest
observed response, what the outlier component is for, and why its scale is a
constant rather than a \code{dpar}.
}
\section{What the heavy tail is for}{
The outlier component behind \code{poutlier} is a half Normal, which by
construction cannot explain a \emph{slow} response: its density at 5 s is
effectively zero, so a long right tail is the decision family's own business.
\code{\link[=cogmod_loggamma]{cogmod_loggamma()}}'s \code{shape} and \code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian]{cogmod_invgaussian()}}'s \code{sigmadrift} are
two ways of providing one. \code{dof} is a third, and the most direct: it absorbs
slow contaminants into the likelihood rather than into a mixture component.
At \code{dof = 5} the probability of a decision time beyond 5 s is about five
orders of magnitude larger than the matching LogNormal's.
}

\section{Two things to know before using it}{
\strong{The mean does not exist}, for any finite \code{dof}. \code{E[exp(sigma * T)]} with
\code{T} a Student-t diverges because the t has polynomial tails and \code{exp()}
outruns them - there is no region of the parameter space where this family
has an expectation, unlike \code{\link[=cogmod_logweibull]{cogmod_logweibull()}}, whose mean exists below
\code{sigma = 1}. \code{posterior_epred()} therefore errors rather than returning a
number. The \strong{median is exact}: \code{ndt + exp(mu)}. For anything else,
summarise \code{posterior_predict()} draws.

\strong{The density is unbounded at \code{ndt}.} As \code{RT} approaches \code{ndt} from above
the decision density grows like \verb{1 / (t * |log t|^(dof + 1))}, where a
LogNormal decays to zero. The spike is integrable for every \code{dof > 0}, so
the posterior stays proper, but the likelihood has no maximum and the prior
on \code{ndt} is what keeps the sampler off \code{min(RT)}. This is the same situation
as \code{\link[=cogmod_loggamma]{cogmod_loggamma()}} above \code{sigma * shape = 1}, and the reason
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} is not optional here.

A Student-t is symmetric \strong{on the log scale}, so a small \code{dof} fattens both
tails rather than only the slow one. At \code{dof = 2} some 1.5\% of the decision
distribution falls below 0.05 s, against a LogNormal's 5e-9 - territory
\code{poutlier} also claims, so the two trade off. \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} centres \code{dof}
at 6 with 95\% of its mass between 1.5 and 24, which keeps the fast-side spike
under a tenth of a percent while leaving the slow tail worth having.
}

